<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   canRetrieveWindowContent is on, and it is the one thing in this app's permission surface
   that needs justifying. It is what makes a view identifier readable
   (AccessibilityNodeInfo.viewIdResourceName), and a view identifier is the only signal that
   can tell Instagram's Reels surface from its feed: Reels and YouTube Shorts run inside the
   app's own main activity, so the foreground package and the window class name are identical
   to the feed's, and a scoped target would never fire without it.

   What is read is a view id and nothing else — no text, no content descriptions, no images —
   and only for a package the user has scoped, only as a yes/no against markers this app
   supplies. canRetrieveWindowContent has no runtime counterpart, so this file is the only
   place it can be set: it is a capability the system parses at bind time, and the service
   therefore edits the info it is handed in SonderAccessibilityService.onServiceConnected
   rather than assigning a fresh AccessibilityServiceInfo over it and losing it.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows|flagReportViewIds"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="50" />
